</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="Sx4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Experiments</h2>

<section id="Sx4.SSx1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection">Setup</h3>

<section id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dataset.</h5>

<div id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To comprehensively evaluate our method, we select five diverse benchmarks covering a wide spectrum of tasks. For reasoning, we use GSM8K <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al. 2021</a>)</cite> for mathematics and TheoremQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al. 2023</a>)</cite> for scientific theorems. For broad knowledge and multi-task understanding, we include MMLU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al. 2020</a>)</cite>. For reading comprehension, we use the English subset of Belebele <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bandarkar et al. 2023</a>)</cite>, and for multilingual generalization, we include MGSM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al. 2022</a>)</cite>. Detailed descriptions of each dataset are available in the Appendix.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Model.</h5>

<div id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our method on five models from two categories: Instruction-Tuned Models (Llama2-7B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Touvron et al. 2023</a>)</cite>, Llama3.1-8B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">MetaAI 2024</a>)</cite>, and Qwen1.5-7B-Instruct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qwen-Team 2024</a>)</cite>) and Long Reasoning Models (LRMs) (DeepSeek-distilled-Llama-8B and DeepSeek-distilled-Qwen-7B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">DeepSeek-AI 2025</a>)</cite>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baseline.</h5>

<div id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare our method against seven baselines grouped into three categories. The first includes common uncertainty metrics: Maximum Softmax Probability (MaxProb) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks and Gimpel 2017</a>)</cite>, Perplexity (PPL) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Si et al. 2023</a>)</cite>, and Entropy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al. 2025</a>)</cite>. The second covers calibration-based methods: Temperature Scaling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shih, Sadigh, and Ermon 2023</a>)</cite> and Energy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al. 2020</a>)</cite>. The third consists of two recent representation-based scores: CoE-R and CoE-C <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al. 2025</a>)</cite>. Mathematical formulations for all baselines are provided in Appendix.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation Metric.</h5>

<div id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.SSx1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We frame hallucination detection as a binary classification task, labeling each response as correct or hallucinated via exact match. We evaluate performance using three standard metrics: AUROC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Boyd, Eng, and Page 2013</a>)</cite>, AUPR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Boyd, Eng, and Page 2013</a>)</cite>, and FPR95 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hendrycks et al. 2022</a>)</cite>. Detailed descriptions of these metrics are provided in Appendix.</p>
</div>
